 17%|█▋        | 17/100 [3:02:37<23:46:09, 1030.96s/it]MLLM description: The Hawksbill turtle is characterized by its distinctive physical features, including a flattened, laterally compressed carapace (upper shell) with a serrated edge, a long, narrow head, and a pair of prehensile front flippers. Its coloration is typically a mottled brown or olive green, with a pattern of dark, irregular markings on a lighter background. The shell has a unique, intricate pattern of radiating lines and scutes (shell plates). Hawksbills are smaller than other sea turtles, with an average length of around 2-3 feet and a weight of about 100-200 pounds. They are known to be solitary animals, often found in shallow waters near coral reefs, where they feed on sponges and other invertebrates. Hawksbills are also known for their unique feeding behavior, using their prehensile front flippers to grasp and manipulate food items. Their unique identifying trait is the presence of a small, hooked spur on each hind flipper, which is used for defense against predators.
